<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marie de France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 1160–1215</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marie de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was a poet of the later twelfth century, probably active in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> at the court of Henry II, and the earliest known woman poet in French. Her twelve Lais, short verse tales of love and the supernatural, are presented as retellings of Breton lays. She also wrote a collection of fables and a version of the legend of Saint Patrick's Purgatory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marie-de-france.jpg" alt="Marie de France" width="320" height="269" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Richard of Verdun · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marie_de_France_1.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
